ds of logics i think you know  in the sort of um you know in the sort  of broader community for example um of  folks  who are sort of you know thinking about  sort of  racial data um and their use and sort of  tech  and algorithms outside of the healthcare  there really has been a lot of sort of  ground swell of community organizing  around  you know being at the table and part of  being at the table isn't just to provide  ideas it really is about  the transparency  of you know what is happening so that it  can be critiqued and shaped so it really  isn't about you know the thing itself or  figuring out how to improve the thing  itself it really is about having these  sort of cultures of transparency and i  think the other way too is you know for  me one of the things reasons why i  wanted to focus  on  the things that workers know  is that we often don't know the things  that workers know because we never  bother to ask them because we don't  really care  if there's carrying out these stats  right but what if we did care right what  if you know we spent time right asking  them to help us understand how  some of the applications that are being  developed  may help them to better do their  job of their as they define it which is  usually not the efficient doing of the  work it's about providing quality care  and that really is a value right and  that's a value that is increasingly sort  of squeezed out of health care care  is increasingly absent and so if you  have  an absence right and a pulling out of  every shred of care under the guise of  efficiency  and sort of getting rid of individual  level variation and therefore bias right  um  you know you're you're not going to be  able to  create algorithms that actually are  going to reproduce the values of care  so  you talked a little bit about the past  informing the future  what sort of steps would you like to see  if they could begin today clinicians in  the healthcare  industry uh industrial complex uh what  could they do today to help start  mitigating any harm and disparity that  these algorithms are kind of uh you know  producing  yeah i mean i think that if you're  talking about the sort of level of  mitigation um you know there are  probably sort of more practical things  on that can that can happen you know  i've talked about transparency um i also  think that it matters um who's at the  table um so you know again you know one  of the reasons why you know i sort of  focused on what it is that workers know  um  is that i don't actually think we have  enough sort of bread and butter  providers um at the table um in the sort  of development of these applications um  you know i think we have providers who  are involved but they tend to be  involved as entrepreneurs  um right  they don't tend to actually  be involved um simply um as a key  stakeholder into providing sort of  community care um  and so i really think that if you took  seriously um the experience that workers  have um sort of navigating this terrain  and ask them you know how  what would um a tool look like when what  would a sort of algorithm look like that  would make your work as you define it  and as you value it to be right what  would that look like um  because i think right now we are solving  problems as defined by employers  we're not solving problems as defined by  workers  and patience so if you ask patients and  and providers to talk about what  problems they want to have solved you  would come up with a very different kind  of end tool than if you asked employers  oh i'm sure you're right and another  thing which kind of  as you were thinking speaking i was  thinking about  the role of of trust and that the  embedding trust into the algorithms  which kind of made me think about what  can we do to encourage people of color  and from you know communities who are  not otherwise well represented in ai and  machine learning and algorithm  development how we encourage them to be  more participatory what can we do to  encourage uh people of color to you know  become coders or to take part in in  these discussions what can we do better  you know as you asked this question i  had a thought that i've never had before  um and one of them because i think you'd  often write this sort of quest to sort  of you know the sort of standard idea is  you know  encourage people to come to  community-based meetings and to sort of  ask for their input um and i think the  other thing is to sort of you know  encourage people to sort of enter in  to the terrain um through  um you know being employed by a  particular company develop a developing  ai or a particular lab developing ai you  know um  and those often are problematic right  because on one hand it is true that  having more diverse people around the  table um you know has the potential to  sort of create more um diverse ideas you  know one of the the difficulties and  problems with always asking for example  lay community members to sort of show up  um is that you know  the experts at the table they're getting  paid for their time the community  participants are not  right  they're ways in which right  always being asked to sort of be  involved in your spare time on one hand  is nice but on the other hand is the  burden yeah sure and i think the second  you know issue with sort of you know you  know maybe having sort of murderous  first faces sort of employing these  organizations is  if i'm an employee of an organization i  may look like the community right and i  might try my best right to sort of speak  on behalf of the community but i am also  a worker for this organization  right um and so one of the things that i  think would be an interesting um um you  know think that you know maybe it exists  um i know that we have um um some some  perhaps some organizers our people  who've been involved in sort of  community-based work in the audience um  is you know  allowing the community to pick an expert  to work on their behalf  one who was accountable to them and not  to an employer  but who has the expertise and the time  to sort of show up and do the work  i think that to me that would actually  be um a way of having  community input from these various  diverse communities  in a way that's respectful of their time  and for the person has independence on  autonomy as well as the expertise to  sort of speak on their behalf  latonya thank you so much we're at the  top of the hour and this has just been a  delightful presentation it's been  wonderful to talk to you and to to hear  your thoughts and to hear your vision i  i just thoroughly enjoyed listening to  you today and uh i'm sure that i'm  speaking for all of our participants and  everybody who will watch this moving  forward thank you so much um we really  appreciate it and to everybody who is  listening uh thank you so much and uh we  will wish you a great weekend and we'll  see you next week thank you
 

